%[text] # The Anonymous Pendulum: Simulating a Parameterized Model
%[text] ## Problem
%[text] **Given:** Pendulum model
%[text] ![](text:image:4c5c)
%[text] ![](text:image:32c1)
%[text] where $\\theta(t)$is the solution to the ODE model.
%[text] **Challenge:** 
%[text] Simulate the pendulum motion for **a range of length values** $L = \[0.1, 0.3\] \\, \\text{m}$, to investigate the affect of the length parameter on the pendulum motion.
%[text] How does increasing the length of the pendulum change the natural frequency of oscillation?
%[text] ## Static: Original, Non-Parameterized, Solution
%[text] Formulate the second-order equation of motion as a system of two first-order equations by introducing a new variable for angular velocity, $\\omega(t) = \\dot{\\theta}(t)$.
%[text] The state vector is
%[text] $\\: \\mathbf{x}(t) = \\left\\{ \\matrix{\\theta(t) \\cr \\omega(t) } \\right\\}$
%[text] (We can define this with the variables in any order.)
%[text] $ \\left\\{ \\matrix{\\dot{\\theta}(t) \\cr \\dot{\\omega}(t) } \\right\\} = \n\\left\\{ \\matrix{ \\omega \\cr \\frac{-1}{5 m L^2} (k\_t \\, \\theta + 3 m g L \\sin{\\theta}}\\right\\}$
%[text] 
%[text] Until now, we had to included **fixed parameter values inside the rate function,** limiting the applicabilty of our solution.
function dxdt = p_rate(t, xx)
    m = 0.36; % kg
    kt = 0.89; % Nm/rad
    L = 0.21; % m
    g = 9.81; % m/s^2
    dxdt = [ xx(2);
        -1/(5*m*L^2)*(kt*xx(1) + 3*m*g*L*sin(xx(1)))];
end
%[text] And simulate by finding the ode45 solution for non-linear dynamics
x0 = [1 0];
tspan = [0 2];
[tt, X] = ode45(@p_rate, tspan, x0);
figure; %[output:59ee7870]
clf; %[output:59ee7870]
plot(tt,X(:,1),'LineWidth',2) %[output:59ee7870]
xlabel("Time (s)") %[output:59ee7870]
ylabel("\theta (rad)") %[output:59ee7870]
grid('on') %[output:59ee7870]
%[text] With this framework **we cannot change the length paramete without editting the** **`p_rate`** **function.**
%%
%[text] ## Parameterize the problem: Wrap the rate function with an anonymous function
%[text] ### Step 1: Parameterize the rate function
%[text] Write a new rate function that includes the parameter we want to change.   Adding **L** to the input arguments.
function dxdt = p_rate_parameterized(t, xx, L)
    m = 0.36; % kg
    kt = 0.89; % Nm/rad
    L = L; % m
    g = 9.81; % m/s^2
    dxdt = [ xx(2);
        -1/(5*m*L^2)*(kt*xx(1) + 3*m*g*L*sin(xx(1)))];
end
%[text] ### Step 2: Iterate through parameter space
ll = linspace(0.1, 0.3, 5);
figure; clf; %[output:76275845]
for ii = 1:length(ll)
    L = ll(ii);

%[text] ###         Step 3: Within the loop, define a new rate function, compatible with ode45, that uses the new model parameter
    new_rate_function_handle = @(t, xx) p_rate_parameterized(t, xx, L);
%[text] ###         Step 4: Solve this new model
    [tt, X] = ode45(new_rate_function_handle, tspan, x0);
%[text] and plot
    plot(tt, X(:,1), 'DisplayName', sprintf('L = %.2f m', L)); %[output:76275845]
    hold on
end

xlabel("Time (s)") %[output:76275845]
ylabel("\theta (rad)") %[output:76275845]
grid('on') %[output:76275845]
legend() %[output:76275845]
%[text] 

